description:
Rewrite lesson 4.04's ownership with `std::unique_ptr`, so the types say who owns what and no code calls `delete`:

```cpp
struct Enemy
{
    static inline int alive{ 0 };
    std::string name;
    int health;
    Enemy(std::string n, int h) : name{ std::move(n) }, health{ h } { ++alive; }
    ~Enemy() { --alive; }
};

std::unique_ptr<Enemy> makeEnemy(const std::string& name, int health);

class Level
{
public:
    Enemy* spawn(const std::string& name, int health);
    void adopt(std::unique_ptr<Enemy> enemy);
    std::unique_ptr<Enemy> release(const std::string& name);
    int removeDead();
    int count() const;
};

struct CloseHandle { void operator()(int* handle) const; };
using UniqueHandle = std::unique_ptr<int, CloseHandle>;
inline int handlesOpen{ 0 };
UniqueHandle openHandle(int value);
```

- **makeEnemy** creates one with `std::make_unique`.
- **Level** keeps a `std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Enemy>>`. `spawn` creates an enemy and returns a borrowed raw pointer; `adopt` takes ownership of one; `release` removes the first enemy with that name and hands its ownership back (empty if there is none); `removeDead` destroys every enemy whose health is 0 or less and returns how many.
- **openHandle** allocates an `int` holding `value` with `new`, adds 1 to `handlesOpen` and returns it in a `UniqueHandle`. The custom deleter `CloseHandle` subtracts 1 from `handlesOpen` and deletes the int.

